mode: "100644"
text: "# Hook Mechanisms — why hooks work\n\nThis is the difference between this skill and a list of swipe-file templates. Templates (\"The\nsecret to ___ that nobody tells you\") go stale because everyone uses them and they name a\n*mechanism* without earning it. Learn the mechanisms and you can generate fresh hooks forever,\nin any voice.\n\nEvery hook works by creating something the reader must resolve. Below are the core mechanisms:\nwhat each is, **why it works** (the underlying human wiring), when to reach for it, and the\nfailure mode that turns it into noise. Generate across several of these, not one.\n\n> The master rule under all of them: the gap must be **real and resolved by the content.** A\n> mechanism used to bait a click the content doesn't pay off is the fastest way to train the\n> algorithm — and your audience — to scroll past you.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Curiosity gap\n**What:** open a loop — promise an answer you make them stay for.\n**Why it works:** an information gap between what we know and want to know creates a genuine\nitch we're driven to scratch.\n**When:** you have a surprising answer or payoff worth withholding briefly.\n**Example shape:** \"The reason this post outperformed everything else has nothing to do with the\nphoto.\"\n**Failure mode:** vague tease with no real payoff (\"you won't believe what happened\"). Be\nspecific enough to intrigue, withholding enough to compel.\n\n## 2. Contrarian / pattern interrupt\n**What:** say the opposite of what the reader expects.\n**Why it works:** a violated expectation demands resolution, and signals a fresh point of view\nworth hearing.\n**When:** the brand has a real, defensible POV (pull it from brand-profile).\n**Example shape:** \"Posting every day is hurting your account.\"\n**Failure mode:** contrarian for its own sake, or manufactured outrage. The take has to be true\nand defensible, not edgy theater.\n\n## 3. Stakes & cost\n**What:** lead with what's at risk, what went wrong, or what something cost.\n**Why it works:** we're wired to attend to threats and losses more than equivalent gains (loss\naversion). Stakes make attention feel mandatory.\n**When:** there's a real consequence, mistake, or price involved.\n**Example shape:** \"This one line in our contract cost us $40,000.\"\n**Failure mode:** invented or inflated stakes. If the cost wasn't real, don't claim it.\n\n## 4. Specificity & numbers\n**What:** an exact, almost oddly specific number or detail.\n**Why it works:** specificity signals truth (vague = made up) and itself provokes curiosity —\n*why that number?*\n**When:** you have concrete data or a precise detail.\n**Example shape:** \"We deleted 80% of our features and revenue went up 12%.\"\n**Failure mode:** round, generic numbers (\"a lot of people\"), or fake precision. The number\nmust be real.\n\n## 5. Identity / callout\n**What:** name the exact person the content is for.\n**Why it works:** self-relevance — we instantly attend to things that are *about us*.\n**When:** you have a sharp audience segment (from brand-profile).\n**Example shape:** \"If you're a founder doing your own payroll, stop.\"\n**Failure mode:** calling out \"everyone\" — the opposite of self-relevant. Be specific about who.\n\n## 6. In-media-res story\n**What:** drop into a scene already in motion, no setup.\n**Why it works:** narrative transportation — an unfinished moment creates tension we want\nclosed, and stories pull us in faster than claims.\n**When:** you have a real moment, scene, or anecdote.\n**Example shape:** \"It's 11pm and I'm refreshing Stripe for the fourth time tonight.\"\n**Failure mode:** starting with backstory or context instead of the moment. Begin in the action.\n\n## 7. Mistake / negativity\n**What:** lead with the error, the \"don't,\" the failure.\n**Why it works:** negativity bias — we weight and attend to negative information more heavily\nthan positive.\n**When:** there's a real lesson from a real failure.\n**Example shape:** \"I've been writing hooks wrong for three years. Here's the fix.\"\n**Failure mode:** pure negativity with no payoff or lesson. The mistake must lead somewhere\nuseful.\n\n## 8. Transformation / before → after\n**What:** hold up the gap between two states.\n**Why it works:** we're wired for progress and change narratives; the distance between before\nand after creates a pull to learn how it was crossed.\n**When:** you can show a real, believable shift.\n**Example shape:** \"From 200 to 40,000 followers without posting daily. Here's what changed.\"\n**Failure mode:** a transformation so dramatic it reads as a scam. Believable beats impressive.\n\n## 9. Question\n**What:** a sharp, self-relevant question the reader can't help answering in their head.\n**Why it works:** combines an open loop with self-reference; the brain auto-answers a good\nquestion.\n**When:** the goal is comments, or the topic invites opinion/identity.\n**Example shape:** \"What's the one task you open 14 tabs to avoid?\"\n**Failure mode:** generic or yes/no questions (\"Want to grow your audience?\"). Make it specific\nand easy — but interesting — to answer.\n\n## 10. Timeliness / FOMO\n**What:** tie the hook to right now — a trend, a deadline, a moment.\n**Why it works:** relevance plus mild scarcity/urgency raises the cost of scrolling past.\n**When:** there's a genuine timely moment to ride.\n**Example shape:** \"Everyone's switching to ___ this week. Here's why it's mostly hype.\"\n**Failure mode:** manufactured urgency with no real time element. Don't fake the clock.\n\n## 11. Authority / social proof\n**What:** lead with credible proof — a result, a name, a number.\n**Why it works:** we defer to credibility and evidence; proof lowers skepticism before it\nstarts.\n**When:** you have real, usable proof (per brand-profile's proof section).\n**Example shape:** \"After analyzing 1,000 of our posts, one pattern explained the top 5%.\"\n**Failure mode:** vague authority (\"trusted by industry leaders\"). Use specifics that are\nverifiable.\n\n---\n\n## For video, the hook is three channels at once\n\nIn the first 1–3 seconds, the hook is **visual + verbal + on-screen text** simultaneously, and\nit must work **muted**:\n\n- **Visual hook:** motion, a pattern interrupt, a striking or mid-action frame. Often outweighs\n  the words — the first frame stops the thumb.\n- **Verbal hook:** the first spoken words (one of the mechanisms above), no slow intro.\n- **On-screen text:** the written hook for sound-off viewers; carries the gap on its own.\n\nNever open a video with a logo, a slow build, or \"Hey guys, in today's video…\" — those are\nattention donations to the scroll.\n\n## Choosing a mechanism\n\n- **Reach/shareability** → contrarian, story, relatable specificity.\n- **Comments** → question, contrarian.\n- **Saves/authority** → specificity, authority, mistake-lesson.\n- **Conversion/trust** → transformation, authority (with real proof).\n\nWhen stuck, draft one hook each from curiosity, contrarian, specificity, story, and stakes —\nfive different angles on the same content — and let the scoring rubric pick the winner.\n"
